{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's distinguishing evidence is a decoy-injection test, but this tests active item-rejection, not the reliability gain claimed: 'stochastic error cancellation coordinated by the checklist.' A panel could correctly reject a planted decoy (showing active judgment) yet still be *less* reliable than a single expert on the *actual verifiable outcomes*, because the decoy test never measures the panel's error rate or the expert's accuracy on real items. Conversely, a panel that ticks a decoy box could still outperform the expert on real outcomes. The problem asks what evidence distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from compliance that tracks the right answer, and SRC_001 gives evidence of active judgment that is independent of — and cannot establish — a reliability gain over the expert.","counterexample":[{"expert":"wrong on 10% of real items — expert more reliable despite panel showing 'genuine' active judgment","panel":"decoy rejected, but panel still wrong on 30% of real items"}],"target":"5541839e1b81a99cf0d674188b910bca12a8e84b4efa7a556a646403cc7929fb"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues against the panel via a 'reliability paradox' of compliance fatigue, but never identifies the evidence within a single evaluation that would distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance tracking the right answer — the problem's explicit second requirement. SRC_002's mechanism addresses *why* compliance might degrade, but a successful answer must say what observable evidence separates a true reliability gain from coincidentally-correct compliance. The counterconditions (adaptive checklist, simple task) concede cases where fatigue may not arise, yet even in those cases no distinguishing evidence is supplied. This is not a mere omission of out-of-scope material; the problem directly demands this evidentiary identification, and SRC_002 provides none.","counterexample":[{"SRC_002_diagnosis":"fatigue may or may not be present depending on checklist design","observation":"panel ticks all boxes correctly and gets the right outcome","problem_required_distinguishing_evidence":"would compliance still produce the right answer if the checklist were silently shortened or if one item were inapplicable? — absent from target"}],"target":"c41d912a9bfed769d171a4c82f368d99a8535f412e601707df738922a6b03251"}]}